Comparable & Comparator in Java – Complete Interview Notes
Definition:
Comparable and Comparator are used in Java to define object sorting logic.
Comparable → natural ordering (inside the class)
Comparator → custom ordering (outside the class)

Comparable:
Definition:
Comparable is an interface used to define the natural ordering of objects.

public interface Comparable<T> {
    int compareTo(T o);
}

Key Points:
Logic written inside the class
Only one sorting sequence
Modifies the class itself
Used by default by Collections.sort()

Comparable Example:
class Employee implements Comparable<Employee> {
    int id;
    String name;

    Employee(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int compareTo(Employee e) {
        return this.id - e.id;
    }
}

List<Employee> list = List.of(
    new Employee(3, "Ram"),
    new Employee(1, "Sai"),
    new Employee(2, "John")
);

Collections.sort(list);

Output:
1 Sai
2 John
3 Ram

Comparator:
Definition:
Comparator is an interface used to define custom sorting logic externally.

public interface Comparator<T> {
    int compare(T o1, T o2);
}

Key Points:
Logic written outside the class
Supports multiple sorting strategies
Does not modify the entity class
Preferred in real projects

Comparator Example (By Name):
Comparator<Employee> nameComparator =
    (e1, e2) -> e1.name.compareTo(e2.name);
list.sort(nameComparator);

Output:
John
Ram
Sai

Comparable vs Comparator (VERY IMPORTANT)
| Feature          | Comparable   | Comparator    |
| ---------------- | ------------ | ------------- |
| Package          | java.lang    | java.util     |
| Method           | compareTo()  | compare()     |
| Sorting logic    | Inside class | Outside class |
| Multiple sorting | ❌ No         | ✅ Yes         |
| Lambda support   | ❌ No         | ✅ Yes         |
| Real-world usage | Less         | **More**      |

Comparator Methods (Java 8+) – MUST KNOW
1️⃣ comparing()
Comparator<Employee> byId =
    Comparator.comparing(e -> e.id);

Output:
1 Sai
2 John
3 Ram

2️⃣ comparingInt(), comparingLong(), comparingDouble()
Comparator<Employee> byId =
    Comparator.comparingInt(e -> e.id);


✔ Avoids boxing → better performance

3️⃣ reversed()
Comparator<Employee> byIdDesc =
    Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::getId).reversed();

Output:
3 Ram
2 John
1 Sai

4️⃣ thenComparing() (VERY IMPORTANT)
Comparator<Employee> byIdThenName =
    Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::getId)
              .thenComparing(e -> e.name);


Used for multi-level sorting.

5️⃣ nullsFirst() / nullsLast()
Comparator<Employee> byName =
    Comparator.comparing(e -> e.name,
        Comparator.nullsLast(String::compareTo));

Streams Sorting Examples (MODERN INTERVIEWS)
Sort Using Comparable (Stream)
list.stream()
    .sorted()
    .forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.name));

Sort Using Comparator (Stream)
list.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(e -> e.name))
    .forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.name));

Sort Descending Order
list.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getId).reversed())
    .forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.id));

Sort by Multiple Fields (Streams)
list.stream()
    .sorted(
        Comparator.comparing(Employee::getId)
                  .thenComparing(Employee::getName)
    )
    .forEach(System.out::println);

Real-World Spring Boot Usage
Sorting API responses
Pagination sorting
Custom DB-independent sorting
Business rule based ordering

Example:
employees.sort(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary).reversed());

Interview Questions & Answers (Sequential)
Q1: What is Comparable?
Answer:
Comparable defines natural ordering of objects using compareTo().

Q2: What is Comparator?
Answer:
Comparator defines custom sorting logic using compare().

Q3: Which one is preferred and why?
Answer:
Comparator, because it supports multiple sorting strategies and lambdas.

Q4: Can a class implement multiple Comparables?
Answer:
No, only one compareTo() method is allowed.

Q5: Can we have multiple Comparators?
Answer:
Yes, unlimited.

Q6: What happens if compareTo() returns 0?
Answer:
Objects are considered equal in sorting.

Q7: Does Comparator affect equals()?
Answer:
No, unless explicitly implemented that way.

Q8: How sorting works internally?
Answer:
Java uses TimSort (hybrid of merge + insertion sort).

Q9: What is stable sorting?
Answer:
Preserves original order for equal elements.

Q10: How do you sort in descending order?
Answer:
Using reversed().

Tricky Interview Questions (4+ Years)
🔥 Q11: Can compareTo() throw NullPointerException?
Answer:
Yes, if not null-safe.

🔥 Q12: Difference between Comparator.comparing() and comparingInt()?
Answer:
comparingInt() avoids boxing → better performance.

🔥 Q13: Can Comparator be stateful?
Answer:
Yes, but not recommended.

🔥 Q14: What happens if comparator is inconsistent?
Answer:
Unpredictable sorting results.

🔥 Q15: Comparator vs Comparable in Streams?
Answer:
Streams prefer Comparator for flexibility.

🔥 Q16: Can we sort Map using Comparator?
Answer:
Yes, by converting to stream of entries.

🔥 Q17: Why Comparator is functional interface?
Answer:
It has only one abstract method → supports lambda.

🔥 Q18: Does sorting modify original list?
Answer:
Collections.sort() → YES
stream().sorted() → NO (creates new stream)

🔥 Q19: Can we sort immutable list?
Answer:
No, it throws UnsupportedOperationException.

🔥 Q20: Best interview one-liner?
Answer:
“Use Comparable for natural ordering and Comparator for flexible, external, and stream-based sorting.”
